demic now with more on this we're  joined by redmond shannon and our london  bureau so redmond through all the ups  and downs over the past 70 years of her  reign the queen had prince philip by her  side but with his passing last april  she'll mark this milestone without him  how does she seem to be dealing with  that  yes miranda well it just uh it bears uh  repeating of course they were married  for 73 years quite quite remarkable when  you think about it so obviously it's  going to have a huge personal impact on  her the queen doesn't talk much about  how she feels about things so it's hard  to know exactly but undoubtedly she will  be feeling that it's still less than a  year since he passed away last april  she has had health issues herself too  since then spending a rare night in  hospital in october also seen wearing or  using a cane  at an engagement in october but uh still  fulfilling engagements although  cancelling some after that hospital stay  she received people for engagements at  buckingham palace but perhaps she is  steadying herself for what will be this  big jubilee year ahead  and remin the queen has also lost in a  way her grandson prince harry you know  he's moved across the pond to los  angeles stepping down from royal duties  along with his wife former tv actress  meghan markle and all of this happening  less than two years into their marriage  harry megan then did that tell-all  interview on oprah while prince philip  was in the hospital this obviously came  as a big blow to the royals didn't it  yeah it certainly did i think  it is in a way you're right another loss  for the queen having harry so far away  moving initially of course to canada and  then down to california and it's uh  pretty much exactly two years since that  announcement from harry and megan to  move away from their royal duties and  just under a year since that was more or  less made permanent by the royal family  as you mentioned there was that tell-all  interview with oprah and harry really  cited two reasons from his point of view  why he wanted to do this first of all  the press he grew up in the spotlight of  the press with his mother diana of  course he said he saw the way things  were going in other words he didn't want  the same thing to happen to him and his  family and he mentioned a lack of  support from the firm the royal  establishment so that obviously would  have hurt the queen blind side of the  family in some regards harry losing his  military appointments and patronages  will certainly hurt him as a a veteran  of the afghan war  the queen of course saying in the  statement that she was saddened by his  move but perhaps she'll be most saddened  of all by what appears to be the falling  out between her two most prominent  grandsons the brothers william and harry  and it doesn't end there redmond the  latest crisis the queen has had to deal  with very much on a public stage as with  everything else is her son prince  andrews fall from grace so what kind of  impact is this having on the monarchy  well it's already had a large impact  obviously with andrew being stripped of  his titles quite recently  uh if this civil case uh lodged by uh  virginia du fray goes ahead in new york  this fall  this will be a media circus miranda it  will be hugely damaging to the royal  family if it does go ahead  one would imagine that ideally from a  royal family point of view this would be  settled outside court but perhaps that  is not what what virginia roberts dufrey  wants she wants to see  prince andrew face some sort of justice  from her point of view prince andrew  trying to defend himself from his  apparent friendship with uh sex  offenders jeffrey epstein and uh and  gillane maxwell  it's there's no good side to this for  the royal family and it has been and  will continue to be hugely damaging all  right remin very quickly i want to ask  you you know these challenges for the  queen go far beyond her family you know  with more and more countries questioning  their ties to the british monarchy and  whether or not it's still relevant so  what do you think this means for the  royals and their future  well they're still of course apart from  the uk are 14 more countries that are  realms including canada barbados leaving  last year and becoming a republic it's  quite difficult for a country like  canada to become a republic for various  reasons  australia tried to do it uh more than 20  years ago it failed the referendum  failed arguably a flawed process  according to some republicans new  zealand tried to do what canada did by  removing the union jack from its flag a  few years ago it couldn't do so so  there are  movements in many countries to move away  from the royal family but there are  still strong connections too and i think  that will continue to be the case at  least as long as queen elizabeth is on  the throne when charles arrives well  that's a different matter  alright redmond thank you so much for  your time today  the queen is no stranger to canada  making 22 visits during her reign she  took her first tour in 1951 when she was  princess elizabeth since then she has  traveled to all regions of the country  and has been a witness and active  participant in many historic events in  1982 the queen traveled to ottawa to  sign canada's constitution securing the  country's independence from britain her  most recent visit was in the summer of  2010. now with more on canada's  constitutional monarchy and what the  future may hold for this country's  relations with the crown we're joined  now by modern british historian marielle  grant so marielle this country has  always had a very strong relationship  with the united kingdom you know we  share a sovereign we're amongst the  oldest parliamentary democracies in the  world and many of our institutions and  systems are modeled on those of great  britain so tell us a little bit more  about what makes us a constitutional  monarchy  well the key thing that makes us a  constitutional monarchy is that the  queen is our head of state  and uh she is technically legally the  queen of canada and of course that's a  product of our colonial past and it  could be as we go forward in the future  as canadians we consider the legacy of  colonialism  we may at some point want to change that  relationship but it is as you said a  very long-standing relationship changing  it would be very very complicated  uh because it would require the  agreement of all the provinces to a  constitutional amendment and as well  there are many groups in this country  that actually have a very close uh  personal relationship with the crown uh  notably indigenous peoples who hold uh  treaty rights with the crown  and arguably there are strong benefits  to having a constitutional monarchy  because it means that we have a head of  state who is politically neutral who can  represent all of us at all times  regardless of our ethnic backgrounds  regardless of our political affiliations  and so arguably having a constitutional  monarchy has its benefits  and 70 years is a long time to be on the  throne how have canada crown relations  evolved over the decades since the queen  ascended to the throne back in 1952  well i think they've evolved in the  sense that  we are an important component of the  commonwealth and the queen has dedicated  most of her reign to  developing the commonwealth into an  institution that moves beyond and past  its colonial antecedents  obviously the queen has visited canada  more than any other country in the  commonwealth she sees herself as having  close bonds with this country and its  people  members of the royal family have served  as governor generals in the past in  canada i suppose the major change that's  taken place really over the course of  the 20th century into the 21st is that  the queen's representatives in canada  the governor generals and lieutenant  governors have now all been drawn from a  canadians or are all canadians what is  the the current role of the british  monarchy in canada then and what do you  think makes this country's bond with the  royals so unique  well obviously as i said a moment ago  we're in a constitutional monarchy so  the queen is our head of state and that  means that she represents all of us uh  on national occasions  she is the one who effectively is giving  out the medals  uh arguably this means that uh the  political leaders in our country uh have  obviously a very important political  role but they are not acting as head of  state  she is  and  she has played an important role in in  the past in terms of  advising um  what  for 12 canadian prime ministers over  time  she has participated in all sorts of  national  activities for example opening the  olympic games in montreal in 1976.  she was here of course to the  repatriation of the constitution in 1982  uh generally speaking uh from a  historical perspective she has tended to  generate a very positive response  uh very famously when she came to quebec  in in the 1960s there was a great deal  of protest and  they uh  people indicated that that when she was  driving through the streets they were  going to be turning their backs to the  uh  to the royal entourage and she was later  heard to comment that she noticed some  of them peeking over their shoulders to  have a look  now we are looking at a time where  people are questioning the relevance of  the monarchy in fact late last year the  government of barbados decided to remove  queen elizabeth ii as its head of state  officially becoming a republic within  the commonwealth so if you sort of  quickly sum up what do you think this  means for the future of canada's  relationship to the royals are we likely  to follow suit of what barbados did  be very very difficult for canada to  sever its relationship with the crown  and become a republic because in order  to do that there would have to be a  constitutional amendment and that would  require  the  consent of all of the provinces and as  well there are certain groups in this  country who have a very important  personal relationship with the crown  uh indigenous peoples who have treaty  rights with the crown and their views on  this would have to be taken into  consideration and one of the advantages  of constitutional monarchy is that we do  have a neutral head of state  interestingly barbados of course has uh  severed its uh constitutional  relationships with the crown at the same  time however barbados has chosen to  remain in the commonwealth of which the  queen is ahead so  this suggests that  uh its move towards uh republicanism is  obviously a reaction to the colonial  past in an attempt to move in another  direction  but uh  at a certain level they still want to  remain uh involved in the commonwealth  and retain some kind of relationship  with the queen  marielle thank you so much for your time  today we appreciate it  thank you  the queen's platinum jubilee marks the  70th anniversary of her accession to the  throne this impressive milestone never  before reached by any other british  monarch will be celebrated with all the  pomp and circumstance befitting a queen  now here to give us a breakdown of what  is being billed as a blockbuster tribute  is royal commentator richard  fitzwilliams he joins us now from london  so richard this is an extremely exciting  time but while february 6th is the  official date of the platinum jubilee  the celebrations won't actually take  place until this june so why is that why  do people have to wait for the  celebrations  the idea is the weather i would point  out i don't want this to appear dampener  but for the coronation in june 1953 it  was also chosen for the weather and it  poured but that didn't dampen people's  spirits because what was being  commemorated was so unique and it's  unique here because as you say  queen victoria had a diamond jubilee the  queen had that in 2012. now this is a  four day extravaganza  which commemorates something  absolutely unique but also there's so  many  fascinating mixed parts in this firstly  you've got what we always have the  beacons which are lit around the  commonwealth and in britain to  commemorate something that again is a  milestone these are beacons they date  back to the days of the spanish armada  where they were warning against invaders  now this of course is celebratory  also the queen's official birthday she  has two it's her prerogative and a  rather fascinating one the parade of  trooping the color which has  nearly 2 000  soldiers and musicians a fabulous  spectacle that kicks it off  all right amazing and the british  government richard has said that it is  promising a once in a generation show  that mixes really the best of british  ceremonial splendor with cutting-edge  technological displays now on top of all  of this you know we know that there's an  actual official metal that is designed  to mark each jubilee so do we know what  the platinum medal will look like and  who exactly is going to be awarded to  we know that the platinum medal is  likely to be awarded to those who've  given a certain level of service  including it has to be said some members  of the royal family i mean this is  something that  tends to be  given to those  particularly those in the armed forces  and of course their various the front  line the pandemic so forth that there  are those who always remember that this  occurred and the medal is there along  with the three previous jubilee medals  of course they tend to have this and the  medal that we've seen the coin uh has  the queen  is saluting or the queen in trooping  with trooping the color which was always  a very iconic image i mean the queen  with tripping the color which i  mentioned is going to be in the first  day the queen is in fact going to be  looking back on  trooping the color which basically go  back to the 1940s and until 1976 she  rode on a particular horse